        "field": "extensions[\"com.integraledger.legal-context\"]",
        "carrierTypes": ["sha256"]
      },
      "expected": false
    },
    {
      "name": "a manifest-level writeCondition validates — the gate ACP's closed session schema forces",
      "$comment": "The declared-extension placement, where the top-level field IS the reference field. v1.37 §C.2 and ACP's own extensions documentation (agenticcommerce.dev/docs/concepts/extensions, read 2026-07-30): a checkout session RESPONSE reports the extensions active for that session at capabilities.extensions[], as objects carrying name / extends / schema / spec, and the declaration whose `extends` names `$.CheckoutSession.legal_context` is what authorizes that field. `CheckoutSessionBase` is additionalProperties:false, so writing it into a session that declares nothing has stock ACP reject the whole session. Because the field IS the placement, an unmet gate leaves nothing placed and `place` refuses. The gate rides a tagged array while the reference is an object-path — a DIFFERENT container kind — which is why writeCondition.container is required rather than inherited. WHY NOT MASTERCARD VI: its constraints specification (verifiableintent.dev/spec/constraints/, read 2026-07-30) says 'Constraints appear ONLY in Autonomous mode credentials', puts the constraints array solely in Autonomous L2 open mandates, and states 'Constraints do NOT appear in Immediate mode credentials (vct: \"mandate.checkout.1\" and vct: \"mandate.payment.1\")' — the only two non-open values — while in the open ones 'Regardless of strictness mode, verifiers MUST reject open mandates containing unknown constraint types'. No value of vct admits a custom LCP constraint, so VI is not a consumer of this axis and v1.37 §C.7's Tier A is drift owed back to the spec — the host wins.",
      "input": {
        "protocol": "acp",
        "pattern": "http-advisory",
        "tier": "A",
        "encoding": "reference-object",
        "container": {
          "kind": "object-path"
        },
        "field": "legal_context",
        "writeCondition": {
          "path": "capabilities.extensions[name=com.integraledger.legal-context].name",
          "container": {
            "kind": "tagged-array",
            "at": "capabilities.extensions",
            "tagField": "name",
            "tag": "com.integraledger.legal-context",
            "valueField": "name"
          },
          "permits": ["com.integraledger.legal-context"]
        },
        "carrierTypes": ["sha256"]
      },
      "expected": true
    },
    {
      "name": "an ALIAS carries its own writeCondition — ACP's negotiated-extension carrier",
      "$comment": "The SAME gate on the SAME field as the case above, but as `placement-acp` will ship it: an alias beside the unconditional metadata.legal_context. The condition sits on the ALIAS and not on the manifest because ACP's metadata carrier requires nothing of the counterparty, so an unmet gate must leave that placement standing rather than refuse it. The gate searches the active declarations for our identifier — a tagged array whose valueField IS its tagField, the entry that identifies itself, so the reader can only ever return the tag and `permits` must list it. Namespace is the deployment's own reverse-domain.",
      "input": {
        "protocol": "acp",
        "pattern": "http-advisory",
        "tier": "A",
        "encoding": "lcp-string",
        "container": {
          "kind": "object-path"
        },
        "field": "metadata.legal_context",
        "readAlso": [
          {
            "path": "legal_context",
            "encoding": "reference-object",
            "write": true,
            "writeCondition": {
